---
/*
 * Section 3 — what happens after you write. Copy verbatim from the approved build brief.
 *
 * THE THIRD BLOCK IS CASL WORK, NOT DECORATION, and the brief says so in as many words.
 * It is the visible statement that emailing ArkSystems does not become implied consent to
 * be marketed at. Canada's Anti-Spam Legislation treats an enquiry as the start of a
 * business relationship, and this is the page that says plainly what we do NOT then do
 * with the address. DO NOT CUT IT FOR LENGTH, and the Privacy link must resolve — it
 * points at /privacy, which is live.
 *
 * "USUALLY WITHIN ONE BUSINESS DAY" IS THE RESOLVED WORDING, not a hedge left in by
 * accident. The approved copy carried an open choice between a hard commitment and this;
 * it was resolved on the hedged version because a hard one is a promise broken by a
 * single bad week, and the standing rule is to make no claim that needs a disclaimer to
 * be true. "Usually" is true every day of the year. Do not tighten it to "within one
 * business day".
 *
 * NO CTA. The section removes a reason not to write; asking for a booking in the middle
 * of it would undercut the paragraph directly above that promises no unrequested sales
 * follow-up.
 */
---

<section class="section section--surface expect">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section__head">
      <h2 class="section__title">What happens after you write.</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="expect__list">
      <li class="expect__item">
        <p class="expect__body">
          <span class="expect__term">A person replies.</span> Usually within one business
          day. Not an automated acknowledgement, and not a sequence.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li class="expect__item">
        <p class="expect__body">
          <span class="expect__term">No sales follow-up you didn&rsquo;t ask for.</span> We
          answer what you asked. If a call makes sense, we&rsquo;ll suggest one once. If you
          don&rsquo;t want one, that&rsquo;s the end of it.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li class="expect__item">
        <p class="expect__body">
          <span class="expect__term">Your details stay here.</span> What you send is used to
          answer you and nothing else. We don&rsquo;t sell, rent or share contact details,
          and we don&rsquo;t add you to a mailing list because you emailed us. See the{" "}
          <a class="expect__link" href="/privacy">Privacy page</a>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .expect__list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--ark-grid-gutter);
    margin-top: 52px;
  }

  /*
   * Terracotta on the top edge, which is the site's card rule. These are rules of
   * engagement rather than features, and they take the same treatment as the numbered
   * blocks elsewhere: a drawn line and text, no icons.
   *
   * NO ICONS HERE, deliberately. Two of these three are promises about what we will NOT
   * do, and an icon against "no sales follow-up" has to illustrate a negative — which
   * reads either as a shrug or as the thing being denied. Same call as /about's section 5.
   */
  .expect__item {
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 3px solid var(--ark-terracotta);
  }

  .expect__body {
    font-size: var(--ark-body);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-body);
    color: var(--ark-body-ink);
  }

  .expect__term {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    font-family: var(--ark-font-display);
    font-size: var(--ark-h3);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-h3);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ark-ink);
  }

  .expect__link {
    color: var(--ark-olive);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .expect__link:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ark-olive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  /*
   * Rows on a phone, not cards, and the terracotta rule moves from the top edge to the
   * left. Three stacked cards each with a full-width rule above it reads as three
   * unrelated sections; one vertical line down the left of all three reads as one set.
   * Same treatment as the homepage's section 3.
   */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .expect__list {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 22px;
      margin-top: 34px;
    }

    .expect__item {
      padding-top: 0;
      padding-left: 18px;
      border-top: 0;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--ark-terracotta);
    }
  }
</style>
